BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20250726T150000Z
DTEND:20250726T160000Z
DTSTAMP:20261003T143422Z
UID:4sbvehe9d8he5hr2v8jonje2h5@google.com
CREATED:20250603T175806Z
DESCRIPTION:<br><p><a href="https://www.chillyhollowbrewingco.com/" target=
 "_blank"><u>Chilly Hollow Brewing Company</u></a> is excited to announce a 
 unique partnership with the <a href="https://www.clarkehistory.org/mill.htm
 l" target="_blank"><u>Burwell-Morgan Mill</u></a>\, a working 18th-century 
 water-powered grist mill in Clarke County\, to grind barley for an upcoming
  beer release: a traditional British-style bitter. History buffs and beer e
 nthusiasts alike are invited to watch the grind firsthand at 11am on Saturd
 ay\, July 26th at the mill on Tannery Lane in Millwood. </p><br><p>This col
 laboration bridges centuries of craftsmanship\, combining the historic mill
 ing techniques of the Burwell-Morgan Mill with the modern brewing artistry 
 of brewing veteran Chris Jacques\, the head brewer at Chilly Hollow. The re
 sult? A beer steeped in history. </p><br><p>"Being able to honor Clarke Cou
 nty’s rich agricultural and historical roots\, especially in the format of 
 a beer\, is really exciting\," says Jacques. "Working with the Burwell-Morg
 an Mill not only showcases a local landmark\, but it also gives our beer an
  added layer of authenticity and story." </p><br><p>The Burwell-Morgan Mill
 \, owned and operated by the Clarke County Historical Association (CCHA)\, 
 was built between 1782-1785. Revolutionary War hero Daniel Morgan was the f
 irst to oversee the operations of the Mill. For most of its life\, the Mill
  has been a functioning operating mill. CCHA took over the operations of th
 e Mill in 1964 and through several restorations brought it back to its form
 er glory. The Mill operates much in the same way today as it did during the
  late 18th century\, with a 20\,000-pound water wheel turning two 3\,000-po
 und millstones to produce cornmeal and flour. The Burwell-Morgan Mill grind
 s many different grains\, often from local farms and for local businesses. 
 </p><br><p>For this particular grind with Chilly Hollow Brewing\, the mill’
 s wheat stone will be adjusted up enough so that the barley hulls are crack
 ed but not ground into flour.  This will allow the barley to work more effi
 ciently in the brewing process.  </p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260403T000749Z
LOCATION:Burwell-Morgan Mill\, 15 Tannery Ln\, Millwood\, VA 22646\, USA
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Historic Barley Grind
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
